+
+[[configurable-collection-implementations]]
+==== Configuring collection implementation types
+
+The built-in implementation types can be replaced through a Java properties resource on the mapping compilation
+classpath. By default MapStruct reads `mapstruct.properties` from the classpath root when that resource exists. The
+`mapstruct.configurationFile` annotation processor option selects another resource by relative path; an explicitly
+selected resource must exist. These resources are resolved from the mapping compilation classpath, not from the
+annotation processor's own classloader.
+
+Each entry uses the supported interface's qualified name as part of the key:
+
+[source, properties]
+----
+mapstruct.collectionImplementation.java.util.List=com.example.FastList
+mapstruct.collectionImplementation.java.util.Map=com.example.FastMap
+----
+
+The implementation can be declared in the current compilation or generated in a later annotation-processing round.
+It must be a publicly accessible concrete class assignable to the configured interface, preserve the interface's
+unbounded type parameters, and provide a public no-argument constructor that declares no checked exceptions. If it
+also provides a usable public constructor taking one `int`, MapStruct passes the source collection or map size as the
+initial capacity. An applicable object factory still takes precedence.
+
+Unknown properties, unsupported interfaces, empty values, conflicting duplicate entries, and invalid implementation
+types cause compilation to fail. Repeating the same key and value is permitted.
